00:28.400 --> 00:49.660
All right, hello and welcome to Camel Finance. I'm your boy Camel and this week is going to be pretty strange, I think. First of all, look at this, all right, 92% in favor of a rate hike, which is pretty wild because that means either this is correct, which would be pretty normal. Every time this thing's over 85, we typically see that's what happens, so that would make sense.

00:49.660 --> 01:02.800
And then we're going to hike into a strange set of conditions, which I'll show you in just a moment. Or this is going to be one of the very first times, I think, I can't even remember a time really when this was over 85 and they didn't follow the bond market.

01:02.800 --> 01:11.680
So I don't know which one of those would be stranger, to be honest with you. The 10 year has a five handle now, which is pretty wild. It's busting above the top of this range.

01:11.840 --> 01:20.100
Unless this thing is literally about to just do this and then immediately reverse, similarly to what it did back here, we might have to hard invalidate this call.

01:20.200 --> 01:27.000
I don't think we're going to have to because the weekly cycle is topping. As you can see, we haven't got much longer to go here before we get a top here.

01:27.000 --> 01:34.360
It's true of the yearly cycle as well. That is topping also. And of course, the TLT is the inverse. So I don't think we're going to need to do much about this.

01:34.420 --> 01:40.960
I think this is probably that final push. But I mean, if it breaks out and stays above this blue range, then it is an invalidation on the call.

01:41.020 --> 01:46.540
And we are going to be looking for higher yields, which is a very, very strange, like you should see the face I'm pulling.

01:46.540 --> 01:54.720
I'm very confused looking at the moment. But then again, tell that to the bond market, right? If this thing stays up here, then we will be seeing hikes.

01:54.720 --> 01:59.780
At least that's typically what I'd be thinking. To show you what I mean by how strange this all is, some of you might remember this chart.

01:59.860 --> 02:07.820
I've shown it a bunch of times before and updated it a bunch of times. But wherever the two year yields go or wherever the bond market goes, really, the Fed funds rate tends to follow.

02:08.260 --> 02:15.420
But when we update this thing right now, I mean, what is going on here? This time doesn't really look anything like any of the other times, right?

02:15.480 --> 02:20.660
This is actually different this time around. And I asked Google, has the Fed ever hiked during a cutting cycle?

02:20.660 --> 02:29.720
And it has once back in 82, which it did so as an experiment, temporarily targeting M1 money supply rather than the Fed funds rate directly.

02:29.900 --> 02:35.720
But even though they hiked 50 basis points back in 82, they resumed cutting three weeks later.

02:36.120 --> 02:43.220
Now, that is a setup that I think would make sense if we're going to get the bond market say, hey, man, you better hike, right?

02:43.220 --> 02:51.660
You better hike immediately. And then the Fed, which follows the bond market, okay, that's what this is showing, says, okay, Mr. Market, you want to hike?

02:51.820 --> 02:56.180
You've got your hike, right? And then the bond market will go, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, we were bluffing.

02:56.280 --> 03:03.500
We didn't actually want that. Okay, yellow moves down, excuse me, green moves down, and then the Fed funds rate will resume cutting again at the next FOMC.

03:03.500 --> 03:13.320
That's a setup that I could compute in my mind, right? That makes sense. But unless that's what happens here, then I'm going to be honest, I have not been this confused in a very long time.

03:13.460 --> 03:19.180
I really haven't. I seldom say this, but I haven't got a clue what is going on here, okay?

03:19.460 --> 03:26.600
Now, that's good for me because we win or we learn, and I'm certainly about to learn something here, something big.

03:26.600 --> 03:32.800
So, I'm paying attention, okay? Once I've figured it out, once I've learned something, I'll share that learning with you as always.

03:33.040 --> 03:36.860
You know, always a student, never a master, and that's certainly the approach I have always taken.

03:37.120 --> 03:43.100
I think the first thing we've got to do is see whether or not tell that to the bond market actually occurs, okay?

03:43.220 --> 03:46.060
And if it does, then we will be seeing a hike because this thing's above 85.

03:46.060 --> 03:55.740
I think something bad will happen if the bond futures price in this rate and then Walsh digs his heels in and just says,

03:55.840 --> 03:59.600
no, no, no, no, no, we're going to hold the interbank lending rate at another pause.

03:59.700 --> 04:03.340
Then I think you're going to get the bond vigilantes come out with the torches and pitchforks.

04:03.500 --> 04:08.900
To be honest, the more I look at this, the more it feels like they're already out there with their torches and pitchforks.

04:08.980 --> 04:12.580
But it could well be that. It could well be as simple as tell that to the bond market,

04:12.580 --> 04:17.720
and the Fed will just simply say, okay, Mr. Bond market, you want a hike? You've got a hike. Here's your hike.

04:18.160 --> 04:23.180
I have a sneaky suspicion if they do that, the bonds will top into their weekly and yearly cycles,

04:23.660 --> 04:26.380
or excuse me, the yields will top into their weekly and yearly cycles,

04:26.560 --> 04:32.640
and the bonds will bottom into their multi-year and weekly cycles,

04:32.640 --> 04:35.040
and then all will be right with the world.

04:35.320 --> 04:38.720
I'm sure I'll hear a lot of I told you so's over the next couple of weeks,

04:39.020 --> 04:42.300
but then I think cycles will do cycle things before too much longer.

04:42.300 --> 04:45.040
Those people that say, I told you so, we'll go quiet again.

04:45.540 --> 04:47.720
And how many times have we seen that? Plenty.

04:48.000 --> 04:50.380
So I think, I think that would kind of make sense.

04:50.660 --> 04:52.980
But there's just so many wild cards. Like, what the heck is this?

04:53.020 --> 04:55.940
If you actually look at this for a moment, like, what is going on here?

04:56.140 --> 04:59.300
It's very, very strange. Kind of seems like the orange has got to catch this up.

04:59.420 --> 05:01.940
And then, you know, are we going into another one of these regimes?

05:02.280 --> 05:04.040
Are we just going to be hiking and hiking and hiking?

05:04.260 --> 05:05.120
I don't think so.

05:05.300 --> 05:08.520
People already can't afford mortgages and loans and everything else,

05:08.720 --> 05:11.280
let alone with a much higher 10-year, okay?

05:11.280 --> 05:15.220
The 10-year is already at 5%, and that puts mortgages in the 7% to 8% neighborhood.

05:15.580 --> 05:17.440
And if we bust up for another couple of percent,

05:17.580 --> 05:19.880
like, people are not going to afford 10% mortgages, right?

05:20.500 --> 05:21.240
You know what I mean?

05:21.640 --> 05:23.220
Something's got to go bang. Something's got to break.

05:23.340 --> 05:26.580
Something's got to go completely differently than how it's going at the moment.

05:26.740 --> 05:27.540
So, I don't know.

05:27.600 --> 05:30.240
Like I said, I'm going to use this as a big, big, big opportunity

05:30.240 --> 05:31.960
for me to learn something here.

05:32.020 --> 05:34.460
And then, like I said, I'll share that with you once I've figured it out.

05:34.460 --> 05:38.500
I do know that the Fed is going to buy about 15.6 billion of bills

05:38.500 --> 05:40.460
for mortgage-backed security reinvestment,

05:41.200 --> 05:43.980
attempting, of course, to keep a lid on that 10-year,

05:44.280 --> 05:46.380
because the Fed buying bills is basically the Treasury

05:46.380 --> 05:49.440
using the proceeds to repurchase 10- and 20-year notes.

05:49.640 --> 05:52.480
So, they are going to try to contain this 5% 10-year.

05:52.680 --> 05:53.680
Note, this is not QE.

05:53.840 --> 05:55.460
It's more like routine balance sheet management.

05:56.400 --> 05:58.200
But, of course, the big question at the moment is,

05:58.300 --> 05:59.180
is it going to work?

05:59.380 --> 06:00.960
Are cycles going to do cycle things,

06:00.960 --> 06:02.820
or are we going to have to retire the channel?

06:04.560 --> 06:06.100
Speaking of retire the channel,

06:06.580 --> 06:11.160
Jim Cramer has the same thesis as me right now, right?

06:11.280 --> 06:13.740
Oil down, rates lower, market goes higher.

06:14.040 --> 06:17.260
Maybe this old camel has lost his way.

06:17.380 --> 06:18.660
When you're on the same side as Cramer,

06:18.840 --> 06:20.240
it's important to ask yourself,

06:20.540 --> 06:21.520
have you got a clue?

06:22.620 --> 06:24.440
And the answer, of course, is no.

06:24.900 --> 06:25.980
So, I don't know.

06:26.040 --> 06:26.880
We're going to need a pivot,

06:27.220 --> 06:29.080
or maybe this is the first time

06:29.080 --> 06:30.640
this guy's ever had a good call.

06:31.100 --> 06:32.240
I'll leave that up to you guys.

06:32.400 --> 06:33.480
On a more serious note,

06:33.760 --> 06:36.140
what really matters to me as a trader

06:36.140 --> 06:37.660
